# Git decides whether a file is binary by looking for a NUL byte in its first 8000 bytes, and nothing
# else. An ONNX model opens with printable metadata - the producer name, a version string, the graph's
# node names - so its first 8000 bytes contain no NUL at all and git files it as text. On Windows, where
# core.autocrlf defaults to true, checking it out then rewrites every LF as CRLF: mad.onnx gains 5824
# stray bytes in the middle of a protobuf and onnxruntime fails with "protobuf parsing failed"
# (error code 7). The bytes in the repository are fine; only the working copy is mangled.
#
# Anyone who already has a mangled copy needs to force git to write it again, since git believes the
# file is unmodified and will not touch it on its own:
#
#     del frontend\public\nn\mad.onnx
#     git checkout -- frontend/public/nn/mad.onnx
#
*.onnx binary

# The same trap in waiting, for formats whose headers are also mostly printable or that we may add
# later. Cheaper to list than to rediscover.
*.wasm binary
*.pt    binary
*.npz   binary
*.gz    binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf   binary
